<$fill $name="body">

!! Why it is built this way

* ''Snapshot, not live filters.'' A live filter in the panel would re-run on every keystroke. Areas run in JS (`check.js`) at set moments and the wikitext reads one JSON tiddler.
* ''First match in JS.'' "An item is in the first area that matches" is a set operation over many filters; it is simple in a loop and awkward in wikitext.
* ''The ring is plain DOM.'' It is created and removed around one pointer gesture. TW's widget refresh would re-render under the pointer. Only the tile data is computed from wiki tiddlers.
* ''Action strings run through `invokeActionString`'' in a small widget tree carrying the variables, with `\import` of the engine so hints and marks can use `wf.*` functions.

* ''Live count, one exception.'' `<$wf-live>` is a widget that ignores wiki changes: it evaluates at render and once per 300 ms pause while the filter text changes. A plain `<$list>` would re-run the filter on every keystroke.
* ''Forms edit a buffer.'' Saving uses `$action-createtiddler $basetitle=… $template=… $overwrite="yes"`, which replaces the whole tiddler; closing the form deletes only the buffer.
* ''Datalist by focus.'' `<$edit-text>` cannot set an input's `list` attribute, so `datalist.js` adds it on `focusin`.

!! Things worth knowing

* `wf-days` and `wf-date` work on local calendar days. TW dates (14 or 17 digits) are UTC instants and are converted to the local day.
* Action attributes are computed before earlier actions in the same string run; do not rely on a field set one line above in the same attribute.
* Config tiddler text ends with a newline; the plugin trims it.
* Area and action `wf.filter` fields are plain filter text, so write them without `{{{ }}}`.
* `$action-deletetiddler` skips hooks; a custom "delete" action must clean up related tiddlers itself.
* The click that follows a drag is eaten in the capture phase, so neither the panel nor what is below reacts to it.

